  "worked_solution": "According to the superposition principle, if the functions ${y}_{1}(t)$ and ${y}_{2}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ are constants, then $y(t)={c}_{1}{y}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{y}_{2}(t)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nWe're given that ${y}_{1}(t)={e}^{3t}$ and ${y}_{2}(t)={e}^{5t}.$ Therefore, the following function is also a solution:\n\n$$\ny(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{3t}+{c}_{2}{e}^{5t}\n$$\n\nIf we let ${c}_{1}={e}^{2}$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get:\n\n$$\ny(t)={e}^{2}\\cdot {e}^{3t}={e}^{2+3t}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
